{{ text.intro | safe }}

<h2>2. Data</h2>
<h3>2.1 The keynote catalogue</h3>
<p>The sample is every main keynote held by nine large technology companies between January
2010 and August 2026: Apple (WWDC and product events), Samsung Electronics (Galaxy Unpacked),
Alphabet (Google I/O, Made by Google and other launches), Microsoft (Build, Ignite and device
events), Meta (F8 and Connect), Amazon (AWS re:Invent CEO keynotes and device events),
NVIDIA (GTC, CES and Computex keynotes), Tesla (unveilings, AI days, investor days and
shareholder meetings) and AMD (CES, Computex and launch events). Each event was researched by
hand: local start time and time zone (which decide whether the market could react the same
day), whether an in-person audience was present, and a YouTube video carrying the full
broadcast audio, preferring the company's own channel. Manual decisions—dropping
out-of-scope events, rejecting videos with commentary, dubbed audio or audience-camcorder
sound—are logged in <code>data/catalog/curation_log.md</code>. The catalogue holds
{{ results.n_keynotes }} keynotes, {{ results.n_with_video }} of them with a usable video.</p>

<figure>
<img src="{{ fig('coverage_by_year') }}">
<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Keynotes per year by audience type. The pandemic moved every
company online in 2020–2022; Apple kept pre-recorded keynotes afterwards, which removes its
recent events from the live-audience sample.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>2.2 From audio to applause</h3>
<p>Each video's audio track (the original-language track; machine-dubbed and "stable volume"
variants are excluded) is decoded to 32 kHz mono and passed through PANNs
<code>Cnn14_DecisionLevelMax</code> (Kong et al., 2020), a convolutional network trained on
the 527 classes of Google's AudioSet. The network outputs a probability for every class every
0.32 seconds; the pipeline keeps thirteen classes (speech, applause, clapping, cheering,
laughter, music and others) averaged to one row per second, stored as a
{{ "%.0f"|format(timeline_kb) }} kB file per keynote. The clap score of a second is
max(P(applause), P(clapping)).</p>
<p>Two steps turn this timeline into keynote-level measures. First, the <em>keynote span</em>
keeps the speech-dominated stretch of the video, which removes countdown pre-rolls and
post-show music, and it is cut where a closing live-music performance begins (Apple
booked musicians for seven of its keynotes). Second, an <em>ovation</em> is a run of seconds
whose clap score stays above an offset threshold, reaches an onset threshold at least once
and lasts at least a minimum duration. The primary measure is <b>applause share</b>, the
fraction of keynote seconds spent in ovations; secondary measures are ovations per minute,
the longest ovation, the mean clap probability (threshold-free) and the share of
cheering.</p>

<figure>
<img src="{{ fig(timeline_fig) }}">
<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> {{ timeline_caption }}</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>2.3 Validating the detector</h3>
<p>Many official uploads carry captions written by professional captioners, who mark
audience reactions with tags such as <code>[Applause]</code> or <code>[Cheering and
applause]</code>. These tags are an independent, human label of when the audience clapped.
Of the videos with human captions, {{ validation.dev_ids|length }} were used to choose the
episode thresholds (onset {{ params.on }}, offset {{ params.off }}, minimum
{{ params.min_dur }} s) by maximising episode-level F1 with a 2-second tolerance, and
{{ validation.test_ids|length }} were held out. On the held-out videos the detector reaches
a second-level ROC-AUC of {{ "%.2f"|format(validation.test_auc_pooled) }}, episode precision
of {{ "%.2f"|format(validation.test_precision) }} and recall of
{{ "%.2f"|format(validation.test_recall) }}. Caption tags are incomplete—captioners skip short
or overlapping applause—so measured precision is a lower bound. As a negative control,
keynotes without an in-person audience should show almost no applause, and they do
(Figure 3, right).</p>

<figure>
<img src="{{ fig('detector_validation') }}">
<figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Left: precision, recall and F1 of ovation detection on the
development videos as the onset threshold varies (vertical line = chosen threshold).
Right: applause share by audience type; virtual keynotes are the negative control.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>2.4 Market data</h3>
<p>Daily split- and dividend-adjusted prices and volumes come from Yahoo Finance for the nine
stocks (Samsung Electronics as 005930.KS on the Korea Exchange), the S&amp;P 500, the
Nasdaq-100, KOSPI and the VIX; Fama–French daily factors come from Kenneth French's data
library, and earnings-announcement timestamps from Yahoo Finance. Exchange calendars
(<code>exchange_calendars</code>) give each session's closing time, including early closes and
the Korea Exchange's 2016 move from a 15:00 to a 15:30 close.</p>

<h2>3. Method</h2>
<h3>3.1 Event study</h3>
<p>The event day <i>t</i><sub>0</sub> is the first trading session whose close is after the
keynote starts. A keynote at 10:00 Pacific therefore has <i>t</i><sub>0</sub> on the same
day, while an evening Tesla event, or a Samsung Unpacked in New York (after midnight in
Seoul), has <i>t</i><sub>0</sub> on the next session. Normal returns come from a market model
estimated by OLS over sessions [−250, −30] relative to <i>t</i><sub>0</sub> (at least 120
returns), using the S&amp;P 500 for US listings and KOSPI for Samsung. Abnormal returns are
cumulated over event windows (CAR) and standardised by the estimation-window residual
volatility, SCAR = CAR / (σ̂<sub>ε</sub> √L), which puts a Tesla move and a Microsoft move
on the same scale. Average effects are tested with the cross-sectional t-test, the Boehmer,
Musumeci and Poulsen (1991) standardised test and Cowan's (1992) generalised sign test.</p>

<h3>3.2 Does applause explain the reaction?</h3>
<div class="keep"><p>The analysis plan (<code>docs/analysis_plan.md</code>) was committed to git before any
applause measure was joined to returns. Its primary specification regresses
SCAR[0,+1] on the within-company z-score of applause share with company fixed effects:</p>
<p class="eq">SCAR[0,+1]<sub>i</sub> = α<sub>company(i)</sub> + β · z(applause share)<sub>i</sub> + ε<sub>i</sub></p></div>
<p>Standardising within company compares each keynote with the same company's other
keynotes, so company style, audience culture and audio mixing cannot drive the result. With
only nine companies, conventional clustered standard errors are unreliable, so inference
uses the wild cluster restricted bootstrap (Cameron, Gelbach and Miller, 2008) with Webb's
six-point weights and 9,999 draws; heteroskedasticity-robust (HC3) intervals are reported
alongside. Secondary outcomes and secondary applause measures form two families, each
corrected with the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure.</p>

